=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — that leaf_router_0_1 lost both of its uplinks to the
spines and is cut off — does not exist in the live lab. A full inspection of
every relevant device confirms the network is completely healthy.

leaf_router_0_1's two uplink interfaces (eth0 at 172.16.0.7/31 toward
spine_router_0_0, and eth1 at 172.16.0.11/31 toward spine_router_0_1) are
both administratively and operationally UP, with zero link-down events
recorded since the lab was brought up (Link ups: 0, last: never; Link
downs: 0, last: never). Both eBGP sessions on leaf_router_0_1 are fully
established and exchanging prefixes with their respective spine peers.
spine_router_0_1 holds valid BGP routes to both 10.0.0.0/24 and
10.0.1.0/24, and the routing tables on both leaf routers carry correct
next-hops. A 10-packet ping from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2)
returned 0% packet loss, and a traceroute confirmed the expected four-hop
path: pc_0_0 → leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_1 → leaf_router_0_1 →
pc_0_1. The asymmetry in super_spine's prefix advertisement to
spine_router_0_1 (0 routes received) is a benign AS-PATH loop-prevention
outcome, not a fault, and is irrelevant to intra-pod leaf-to-leaf forwarding.

Root cause: The helpdesk ticket is based on incorrect information — no
uplink failure on leaf_router_0_1 has occurred and no connectivity
impairment exists between the two host subnets.

Corrective fix: No configuration or operational change is needed. Advise
the user that the path is fully functional and ask them to re-test; close
the ticket as "no fault found / false premise."
=== END ===
